<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Kropotkin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1842">1842</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pyotr Alexeyevich Kropotkin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Kropotkin was a Russian anarchist political philosopher and geographer, a proponent of anarchist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. Born into an aristocratic land-owning family, he attended the Page Corps and served as an officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, where he took part in geological expeditions. Imprisoned for his activism in 1874, he escaped two years later and spent 41 years in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He argued for a decentralised communist society of voluntary associations of self-governing communities and worker-run enterprises, in The Conquest of Bread (1892) and Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution (1902). He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1917 but was disappointed by the Bolshevik state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin.jpg" alt="Peter Kropotkin" width="330" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Kropotkin_circa_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pyotr Alexeyevich Kropotkin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 9 December 1842, the youngest of four children. His father, Alexey, was a royal officer who owned serfs in three provinces. His mother died of tuberculosis when he was three, and he and his brother Alexander were raised mostly by their German nurse. In 1862 he chose to serve with the Amur Cossacks in east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, a post that allowed him to travel and become financially independent of his father. There the exiled poet Mikhail Mikhailov introduced him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a> by recommending an essay by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1872 Kropotkin travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where he met the Jura Federation of followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a> and was converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>. In 1879 he started the fortnightly Le Révolté in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, which published his articulation of anarchist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, the idea of distributing the product of work by need. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he was imprisoned for almost four years. He then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> until 1917 and co-founded the monthly Freedom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in late 1886.</p><p>In The Conquest of Bread (1892) he proposed an economy of voluntary cooperation in which everyone could take what they needed from the social product. In Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution (1902) he answered the social thinkers who stressed competition by arguing that cooperation made for the success of species. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in June 1917 and refused the Provisional Government's offer of a cabinet seat. He argued with Lenin against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>' hostage policy and centralisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kropotkin died of pneumonia on 8 February 1921. His family refused an offer of a state funeral, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> permitted a restrained official memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, the last major anarchist demonstration of the period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, since the movement and his writings were suppressed later that year. He had little influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>, but his books and pamphlets were the most widely read of anarchist literature and influenced revolutionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a> and Emiliano Zapata.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Kropotkin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Proudhon’s mutualism called for free contract, federation and a bank of the people; Bakunin’s collectivism for collective ownership and pay by time worked; <b>Kropotkin</b>’s anarcho-communism for distribution according to need; anarcho-syndicalism for unions of workers to run society.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Leninists rely on a vanguard party of professional revolutionaries bound by democratic centralism; libertarian communists favour workers’ councils and self-management; anarcho-communists such as <b>Peter Kropotkin</b> wanted society to pass at once to communism, without a state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Peter Kropotkin</b> argued in Mutual Aid (1902) that the fittest could be those who cooperate.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitrov">Dmitrov</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1893" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter Kropotkin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1893">What was here in 1893? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Don't compete!—competition is always injurious to the species, and you have plenty of resources to avoid it!&#8221;</p><footer>Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution (1902), ch. 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4341/pg4341-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…those species which best know how to combine, and to avoid competition, have the best chances of survival and of a further progressive development. They prosper, while the unsociable species decay.&#8221;</p><footer>Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution (1902), ch. 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4341/pg4341-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The years that I spent in Siberia taught me many lessons which I could hardly have learned elsewhere. I soon realized the absolute impossibility of doing anything really useful for the masses of the people by means of the administrative machinery.&#8221;</p><footer>Memoirs of a Revolutionist (1899) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73882/pg73882-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Have the factory and the workshop at the gates of your fields and gardens, and work in them.&#8221;</p><footer>Fields, Factories and Workshops (1899), conclusion &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64353/pg64353-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;With many large divisions of the animal kingdom mutual aid is the rule.&#8221;</p><footer>Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution (1902), ch. 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4341/pg4341-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But what right had I to these higher joys, when all round me was nothing but misery and struggle for a mouldy bit of bread…&#8221;</p><footer>Memoirs of a Revolutionist (1899), part 4 &#183; original &#183; on giving up geography for the revolutionary cause &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73882/pg73882-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
